{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1907 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5391547fc66d13b82396887dfaea12f78869f4d449e1ed38b7b20d72b284b39f" :edb/db-before-t 1906 :edb/db-after-t 1907 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535219 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:49:39.917Z" 13194139535219 true] [17592186119530 1100 "Given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/98496fe489edc905b587f42825bd0ef76dffa68bece7e204abae6de259501835.png) find its LU decomposition." 13194139535219 true] [17592186119530 1100 "Given $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ find its LU decomposition." 13194139535219 false] [17592186119530 1145 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + 1 \\cdot R_{1}⟺\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535219 false] [17592186119530 1145 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535219 true] [17592186119531 1100 "Given\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$, find its LU decomposition." 13194139535219 false] [17592186119531 1100 "Given \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/4a8b4b988e2f4c93881f577ce12f2aa59a4dbcc00bf4122e23873255c27c2d72.png), find its LU decomposition." 13194139535219 true] [17592186119531 1145 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1}⟺\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535219 false] [17592186119531 1145 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535219 true] [17592186119592 1100 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A$?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535219 false] [17592186119592 1100 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/f07498a45503a02fa9fb4d3ecfd9bdae54a363b632152016f2f4e5c3f6170656.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/300278e3edb64ab9dcb945668b923f385b9873be5bd33264a7ed10f6072262b7.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/e26513d3753cd1437a42a9f57f160fb25e2fb9b84e1df1db07579429749eea2c.png)" 13194139535219 true] [17592186119592 1145 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A$, we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535219 false] [17592186119592 1145 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/98d64291b8e0d4effe6bb1f3006c3d88d2687697355a83c1b092cdfb54ba4188.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/10a251d4445a3b190e92fa179badfb5e989b577f3c88812334e921522e7a20e5.png)\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix b